<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Ribault</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1520">1520</a>–<a href="/years/1565">1565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Ribault was a French Huguenot naval officer who led French attempts to colonise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. Backed by Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspard-de-coligny">Gaspard de Coligny</a>, he explored the coast in 1562 and left a garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlesfort">Charlesfort</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>. In 1565 he brought reinforcements to Fort Caroline, but his fleet was wrecked by a storm, and he and most of his men were killed by the Spanish under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-menendez-de-aviles">Pedro Menéndez de Avilés</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas-inlet">Matanzas Inlet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-ribault.jpg" alt="Jean Ribault" width="223" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Ribault.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jean Ribault was born at Dieppe in Normandy in 1520 and served in the French navy under the Huguenot admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaspard-de-coligny">Gaspard de Coligny</a>, who in 1562 chose him to lead an expedition to found a colony in the New World. Leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on 18 February with 150 colonists, he explored the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-river">St. Johns River</a>, which he named the River May, and set up a stone column claiming the land for France. Further north, at Port Royal Sound, he laid out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlesfort">Charlesfort</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parris-island">Parris Island</a>, named after King Charles IX, and left 27 men there under Albert de la Pierria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">French Wars of Religion</a> begun. He helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> at Dieppe, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> when the city fell, and gained an audience with Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, but was then held in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> as a spy; in England, probably while imprisoned, he wrote an account of the voyage that survives only in English translation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlesfort">Charlesfort</a> meanwhile collapsed after a fire and a mutiny in which de la Pierria was killed, and Coligny gave the next voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-goulaine-de-laudonniere">René Goulaine de Laudonnière</a>, who founded Fort Caroline on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-johns-river">St. Johns River</a> in 1564.</p><p>Delayed by fresh war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Ribault sailed in the summer of 1565 with five ships and 800 settlers, reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> on 28 August and relieved Laudonnière as governor. In early September Pedro Menéndez de Avilés found his ships off the River May and, after a brief skirmish, landed to the south and founded St. Augustine. Ribault sailed to attack him, but the hurricane of 11 September drove his fleet ashore on the Canaveral coast, while the Spanish marched overland and took Fort Caroline on 20 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Several hundred survivors of the wrecks walked north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matanzas-inlet">Matanzas Inlet</a>, where Ribault, believing his men would be fed and decently treated, surrendered. Following the orders of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a>, those who were not professing Catholics were put to the sword; about 350 of his officers and men died in two massacres. In 1568 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominique-de-gourgues">Dominique de Gourgues</a> avenged him by attacking Fort Caroline and killing its defenders. A replica of his column stands at the Fort Caroline National Memorial, and the wreck of his flagship, La Trinité, was located off Cape Canaveral in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean Ribault” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He overwhelmed Fort Caroline, sparing only the women and children, and ordered the shipwrecked Huguenots of <b>Jean Ribault</b>'s fleet executed at the place called Matanzas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Backed by Admiral Gaspard de Coligny, the Protestant captain <b>Jean Ribault</b> sailed to Florida in 1562 and left a small garrison at Charlesfort, on Parris Island in modern South Carolina, which was soon abandoned.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-florida">French Florida</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dieppe, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Fort Caroline, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.93,1.08&amp;year=1549" aria-label="Dieppe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4575 426 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jean Ribault"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5025 696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5025 696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.93,1.08&amp;year=1549">What was here in 1549? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
